{
 "slug": "composite-and-inverse-functions",
 "title": "Composite and Inverse Functions",
 "seo_title": "Year 11 Maths: Composite and Inverse Functions Worksheets (GCSE)",
 "short_title": "Composite and inverse functions",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "KS4: Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/gcse-mathematics-subject-content-and-assessment-objectives",
 "standard_text": "Interpret the reverse process as the inverse function; interpret the succession of two functions as a composite function.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 11 GCSE maths worksheets on function notation, composite functions fg(x) and inverse functions f⁻¹(x). 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "fg(x) means do g first, then f.",
 "key_sub": "The inverse f⁻¹(x) undoes f.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p><b>Function notation</b>: f(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i> + 3 means “double, then add 3”. f(5) = 13.</p><p>A <b>composite function</b> fg(<i>x</i>) means f(g(<i>x</i>)): do g first, then f. Usually fg(<i>x</i>) ≠ gf(<i>x</i>).</p><p>The <b>inverse function</b> f<sup>−1</sup>(<i>x</i>) undoes f. Write <i>y</i> = f(<i>x</i>), make <i>x</i> the subject, then swap the letters.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>f(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i>² + 1 and g(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i> − 3. Find fg(2).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>g(2) = 1</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>f(1) = 2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find f<sup>−1</sup>(<i>x</i>) when f(<i>x</i>) = 4<i>x</i> + 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>y</i> = 4<i>x</i> + 7</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i> = (<i>y</i> − 7) ÷ 4</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: f<sup>−1</sup>(<i>x</i>) = (<i>x</i> − 7) ÷ 4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Doing f first in fg(<i>x</i>). Start with g, the function nearest to <i>x</i>.</li><li>Writing f<sup>−1</sup>(<i>x</i>) as 1 ÷ f(<i>x</i>). The −1 is not a power here.</li><li>Undoing the steps in the same order. Undo them in reverse order.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does fg(x) mean?",
   "a": "It is a composite function: put x into g first, then put the answer into f. So fg(x) = f(g(x))."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you find an inverse function?",
   "a": "Write y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then swap x and y. The result is f⁻¹(x)."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can you check an inverse function?",
   "a": "Put a number into f, then put the answer into f⁻¹. You should get back the number you started with."
  }
 ],
